---
/**
 * What belongs in indoor climate-controlled storage in Georgia humidity.
 *
 * Prices come from src/data/units.ts. Climate units are interior hallway
 * units — never described as drive-up. The sealed container is not climate
 * control. No setpoints, reviews, rankings, or rate-lock claims.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import BlogAside from '../../components/BlogAside.astro';
import { CONTACT, ADDRESS } from '../../data/site';
import { IMAGES } from '../../data/images';
import {
  CLIMATE_UNITS,
  CONTAINERS,
  formatPrice,
  RATE_TERMS,
} from '../../data/units';
import { formatBlogDate, getBlogPost } from '../../data/blog';

const post = getBlogPost('what-to-store-in-climate-controlled-storage');
const chattanooga = getBlogPost('climate-controlled-storage-near-chattanooga');
const container = CONTAINERS[0];
const climateRates = CLIMATE_UNITS.map((u) => `${u.name} at ${formatPrice(u.price)}`).join(', ');

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Climate storage guides', href: '/blog' },
  { name: post.crumb, href: post.href },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={post.documentTitle}
  description={post.description}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  article={{
    headline: post.headline,
    description: post.description,
    datePublished: post.datePublished,
    dateModified: post.dateModified,
  }}
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Climate-controlled storage"
    title={post.headline}
    lede="Indoor hallway units in Rossville for the wood, electronics, paper, instruments and mattresses that Georgia humidity ruins."
    crumbs={crumbs}
  />

  <section class="container-page py-14 sm:py-20">
    <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.6fr)_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-16">
      <article>
        <p class="text-sm text-ink-600">
          <time datetime={post.datePublished}>{formatBlogDate(post.datePublished)}</time>
        </p>

        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          Georgia humidity is what ruins stored goods. Rossville sits in a humid
          subtropical climate: summers are long, warm, and wet. An uninsulated metal
          building tracks that weather. It runs hotter than the outside air in the
          afternoon, then cools at night, and moisture condenses on the coldest
          surface inside — a tabletop, a television, the back of a picture frame.
          Repeated from June into September, that cycle loosens wood joints, blooms
          mildew on fabric, cockles paper, and corrodes electronics from the inside.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          Indoor climate-controlled storage stops the cycle. At {ADDRESS.street} the
          climate-controlled units are inside the building. You walk in a door and
          the units are along a hallway. They are not drive-up. Temperature and
          humidity are both controlled. Catoosa County Storage has been open since
          2001. The office is at {ADDRESS.formatted}, {CONTACT.phoneDisplay}.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 text-headline font-semibold">What heat and humidity do</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          Heat alone does not grow mildew. Relative humidity above roughly 60
          percent is where mildew starts on fabric, leather, and paper, and the
          smell does not wash out. Wood takes on moisture when the air is wet and
          gives it back when the air dries, so across a humid summer and a dry
          winter glue lines open, veneer lifts, and drawers stick. Warm damp air
          on a surface that cooled overnight beads into water, and on a circuit
          board that water is corrosion.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          A climate-controlled unit holds temperature and humidity steadier. We do
          not publish a setpoint. Call {CONTACT.phoneDisplay} if you need the
          current targets. The
          <a
            href="/climate-controlled-storage"
            class="font-semibold text-brand-700 underline underline-offset-2"
          >
            climate-controlled storage page
          </a>
          is the short version of the same point.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 text-headline font-semibold">Wood furniture</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          Solid wood tables, dressers, chairs, cabinets, and pianos are the clearest
          reason to rent an indoor climate-controlled unit. Veneer and particle
          board are more fragile: the face and the core move at different rates,
          and the glue fails first. Take the drawers out, wrap the piece in a
          cotton sheet or furniture pad, and do not use a tight plastic tarp — it
          traps moisture already in the wood. Dry a rain-wet piece in the house
          before it comes in, and pad corners before you stack anything on it.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 text-headline font-semibold">Electronics</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          Televisions, computers, audio gear, and anything with a circuit board
          should not spend a Georgia summer in an uncontrolled unit. Condensation
          sits in connectors, and batteries leak in the heat. Take the batteries
          out, box the device, and keep the box off the floor. Clean and dry
          refrigerators and washers, prop the doors slightly ajar so the inside
          cannot grow mold, and secure the doors so they cannot swing into the
          hallway.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 text-headline font-semibold">Documents and photographs</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          Paper and photographs do not recover once humidity has had them. Prints
          stick to each other and to glass, documents cockle, and ink moves. Tax
          files, business records, medical records, and photographs you cannot
          reprint belong in climate control if they have to leave the house. Take
          photos out of glass frames, store them flat, and skip cardboard that has
          been sitting in a garage. Plastic boxes are fine when the paper goes in dry.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 text-headline font-semibold">Instruments</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          Guitars, violins, pianos, and anything with a wooden soundboard crack and
          go out of adjustment when humidity swings. A case helps. It does not
          replace a controlled room through July and August. Loosen string tension
          a little if the maker says to. Vinyl warps well below the heat an
          uncontrolled metal unit reaches in August, so store records upright,
          packed so they cannot lean.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 text-headline font-semibold">Mattresses and upholstered furniture</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          Fabric and foam hold damp. A mattress that spends the summer in a humid
          unit comes back with mildew in the foam. Clean it, make sure it is dry,
          and use a breathable bag rather than sealed plastic. Do not store one
          that got rained on. Sofas follow the same rule: vacuum them, dry them,
          and cover them with a sheet. Climate control does not dry a sofa that
          went in wet.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 text-headline font-semibold">How the indoor hallway units work</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          You park, walk in a door, and the climate-controlled units are along a
          hallway. Loading is a hand truck or a few trips, not a tailgate at the
          unit door. That access is why these units can hold an interior climate.
          The drive-up units on the same property are a separate product: you pull
          a vehicle to those doors, and they are not climate-controlled. Use them
          for tools, patio furniture, yard equipment, and building materials. Use
          the hallway for wood, electronics, paper, instruments, and mattresses.
          Do not leave goods in the hallway while you sort a load.
        </p>

        <Figure
          image={IMAGES.interiorClimateCorridor}
          sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 44rem, 100vw"
          class="mt-8"
          caption="Inside the climate-controlled building: a hallway of interior units, not drive-up doors."
        />

        <h2 class="mt-12 text-headline font-semibold">Packing so climate control can work</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          Climate control manages the air. It cannot dry a box you sealed wet.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          <li>Dry everything first. One damp rug in a closed bin will mold the bin.</li>
          <li>
            Use breathable covers on wood and upholstery. Use plastic bins for paper
            and small electronics only after those items are dry.
          </li>
          <li>
            Keep boxes off the floor where you can, and leave a gap between the
            stack and the wall so air moves.
          </li>
          <li>
            Leave food, candles, and anything that melts or attracts pests at home.
            Climate control is not a reason to store perishables.
          </li>
          <li>
            Keep a box list. Six months later you will not remember which carton
            holds the photographs.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="mt-12 text-headline font-semibold">The sealed container is not climate control</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          A third option sits alongside the hallway units and is easy to mix up
          with them. {container.fits} It is {formatPrice(container.price)}. Rent it
          when you want a closed, dust-free container and the goods can tolerate
          North Georgia heat and humidity. Do not put photographs, instruments,
          mattresses, or solid wood furniture in it and expect climate-controlled
          conditions. Those belong in the indoor units. Details are on the
          <a
            href={`/units#${container.slug}`}
            class="font-semibold text-brand-700 underline underline-offset-2"
          >
            pricing page
          </a>.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 text-headline font-semibold">What the indoor units cost</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          Indoor climate-controlled rates are {climateRates}. The sealed dust-free
          container is {formatPrice(container.price)}. {RATE_TERMS} A size can be
          full, so call
          <a
            href={CONTACT.phoneHref}
            class="font-semibold text-brand-700 underline underline-offset-2"
          >
            {CONTACT.phoneDisplay}
          </a>
          or
          <a href="/reserve" class="font-semibold text-brand-700 underline underline-offset-2">
            check availability
          </a>
          and we will quote what is open. The
          <a
            href="/storage-unit-size-guide"
            class="font-semibold text-brand-700 underline underline-offset-2"
          >
            size guide
          </a>
          walks through what fits each indoor unit, and the rates sit on
          <a href="/units" class="font-semibold text-brand-700 underline underline-offset-2">
            units and pricing
          </a>:
          <a href="/units#5x10-climate" class="font-semibold text-brand-700 underline underline-offset-2">5x10</a>,
          <a href="/units#10x10-climate" class="font-semibold text-brand-700 underline underline-offset-2">10x10</a>,
          and
          <a href="/units#10x20-climate" class="font-semibold text-brand-700 underline underline-offset-2">10x20</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 text-headline font-semibold">Rossville, Fort Oglethorpe, Chattanooga, and Ringgold</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          The building is at {ADDRESS.formatted}, about a tenth of a mile north of
          Cloud Springs Road (GA-146). {ADDRESS.accessNote} Rossville, Fort
          Oglethorpe, and Ringgold are on the Georgia side. The Tennessee line is
          about a mile north, so south Chattanooga is a normal drive.
          <a
            href={chattanooga.href}
            class="font-semibold text-brand-700 underline underline-offset-2"
          >
            Directions from Chattanooga
          </a>
          are written out separately, and the
          <a href="/location" class="font-semibold text-brand-700 underline underline-offset-2">
            location page
          </a>
          has the same Cloud Springs landmark. Tools can go in a drive-up unit. A
          dresser should not spend a Georgia summer in one.
        </p>
      </article>

      <BlogAside />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="Want an indoor climate-controlled unit?"
    body="Tell us what you are storing. We will say which size fits and what is open."
  />
</BaseLayout>
